{{nihongo|'''Ryuji Takemoto'''|竹本龍二|Takemoto Ryūji}} (1668–1729), famously known in [[Okaiken]] lore as the Drunken Dragon of Kazan ({{nihongo|'''Kazan no Yoi Ryū'''|火山の酔い龍}}) and in early [[Balisca]]n colonial records as '''El Dragón Borracho''' or '''Takemoto el Pirata''', was a tozama daimyō of the Honda Shogunate who became one of the most notorious Sanese pirates of the early eighteenth century. 

Living during the height of the strict isolationist policies established by the fourth shogun, [[Honda Shigemasa]], Takemoto's life was defined by his rebellion against the rigid social order of the ''Pax Honda''. Following a scandalous, alcohol-fueled disruption at the capital, he was stripped of his titles and exiled. His subsequent daring escape from the penal Kazan Islands and his trans-Shendan voyage established one of the earliest documented unofficial Sanese footholds in Southwest Balisca. Today, he is recognized as a highly romanticized anti-hero in Okaiken and a foundational figure contributing a pirate settler thread to the Sanese-Baliscan diaspora.

==Early life and daimyō career==
Born in 1668 into the Takemoto clan, a minor ''tozama'' (outsider) daimyō house with terrestrial holdings in what is now [[Ehime Prefecture]], Ryuji inherited his domain around 1692. He gained a reputation as a highly capable warrior and local administrator during the relatively stable, albeit suffocating, mid-Honda period. He dutifully participated in the arduous ''sankin-kōtai'' (alternate attendance) processions to the capital of [[Okami]] and performed occasional coastal defense duties mandated by the shogunate to enforce the closed-country edicts.

Despite his competence, Takemoto was infamous among his peers for his heavy drinking and volatile temperament. Chronicles describe him as a charismatic but deeply unpredictable figure, frequently consuming massive quantities of sake and shōchū even during official administrative duties. In an era where the shogunate demanded absolute discipline and adherence to strict Neo-Confucian etiquette, Takemoto's behavior was viewed with increasing suspicion by the central authorities.

This tension culminated in the winter of 1702. During a critical audience in Okami under the shogunate's strict protocols, a heavily intoxicated Takemoto allegedly insulted a senior ''fudai'' advisor, disrupted the formal proceedings, and caused a massive public scandal within the inner citadel. The reigning Shogun, Honda Shigenori, viewed this outburst not merely as a breach of etiquette, but as a direct, destabilizing threat to the Great Peace and the moral order enforced under the isolationist laws. As absolute punishment, Takemoto was subjected to ''kaieki''; he was stripped of his title and lands, formally banished from the mainland, and exiled to the remote [[Kazan Islands]].

==Exile and pirate career==
The Kazan Islands operated as a volcanic, storm-battered secondary archipelago traditionally utilized by the Honda shogunate as a harsh penal colony for political outcasts and minor criminals. Takemoto endured roughly three years of grueling exile on one of Kazan's larger, sulfur-scarred islets under minimal guard supervision. 

Refusing to succumb to the desolate conditions, Takemoto leveraged his samurai training, his natural charisma, and forged alliances with local indigenous fishermen and fellow exiles. Around 1706, he orchestrated a bold and violent escape. With a small band of disgraced retainers and islanders, initially estimated at fifty men, he commandeered and secretly refitted a large coastal vessel into a heavily armed raiding junk.

Adopting a black dragon banner to symbolize his given name, Ryūji, Takemoto successfully revived the ancient, terrifying traditions of the Sea Wolves that had plagued the archipelago during the chaotic Kitayama period. His fleet slipped past the shogunate's coastal patrols and began operating in the deep waters of the [[Shendan Ocean]]. For nearly two decades, he built a formidable reputation as a cunning pirate lord. He primarily targeted vulnerable [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] and Kwangju merchant ships, carefully avoiding direct clashes with the Sanese Imperial Navy, which was strictly limited to coastal defense under the maritime prohibition laws. Takemoto thrived by raiding, smuggling contraband luxury goods, and occasionally serving as a highly paid mercenary for opportunistic Fosian traders who chafed under the region's heavy isolationist tariffs.

By the early 1720s, increasing pressure from the exasperated Bakufu coastal fleets and the growing dangers of continued operations near the Fosian mainland pushed Takemoto toward bolder ambitions. Inspired by vague, centuries-old rumors of distant lands beyond the Shendan—echoing the faint fifteenth-century Sanese exploratory voyages that preceded the Great Disappointment—Takemoto prepared a massive, one-way trans-oceanic expedition.

==The trans-Shendan voyage and Baliscan settlement==
In late 1724, Takemoto led a heavily armed fleet of three ocean-going junks carrying approximately two hundred followers. This diverse flotilla included disgraced samurai, disenfranchised peasants, maritime smugglers, and several families including women and children seeking a life beyond the Shogun's reach. 

The Trans-Shendan crossing was a perilous, five-month ordeal marked by devastating typhoons, outbreaks of [[W:Scurvy|scurvy]], and severe navigational challenges across uncharted waters. The battered flotilla finally made landfall in early 1726 along the rugged, wind-swept southwestern coast of the Tinguirirican Peninsula, located in what is now western [[Balisca]].

At the time of their arrival, the region was under the nominal colonial oversight of the [[United Kingdoms of Volisania]], expanding southward from their primary hubs in [[Dagit]]. However, the Tinguirirican coast remained sparsely controlled by Illypnian forces and maintained a strong indigenous presence. The local populations primarily consisted of the Wallche, who utilized advanced agricultural terracing and irrigation, and the Yamana, who were highly skilled maritime hunter-gatherers navigating the fjord-like [[W:Ría|rías]] and freezing channels. 

Takemoto's exhausted group established a fortified, hidden settlement in a deep, sheltered cove. This enclave was initially called Takemoto-no-Sato by the Sanese exiles, and was later rendered in Volisanian colonial records as Puerto Takemoto or [[Cala del Drac]] ("Dragon's Cove"). Under Takemoto's pragmatic leadership, the settlers negotiated tentative, mutually beneficial alliances with the local indigenous groups. The Sanese traded high-quality iron tools, weaponry, and basic agricultural techniques for access to arable land, abundant seafood, and safe harbor. 

This cultural exchange proved highly transformative for the region. The Sanese introduced early capsicum cultivation and preservation methods brought over from Okaiken, alongside rudimentary ship repair facilities. Where the geography permitted, they carved out small rice paddies and utilized local ingredients to begin early sake distillation. Local culinary legends suggest these early, peaceful contacts planted the initial seeds for the spicy-fusion elements that would later heavily influence regional Baliscan cuisine. The community remained small and precarious, never exceeding a few hundred residents at its peak, surviving through a careful mixture of localized piracy, indigenous trade, and adaptation to the harsh, semi-arid coastal geography mixed with deep fjords.

==Death and legacy==
Ryuji Takemoto died around 1729 during a violent skirmish. Historical accounts differ on the exact nature of his final battle; some sources claim he fell defending the cove against Volisanian colonial naval patrols attempting to root out piracy, while others suggest a clash with rival indigenous raiding groups. Sanese oral tradition holds that he faced his end still fiercely defiant, standing on the barricades with a drawn sword in one hand and his signature sake cup in the other.

Following his death, his followers permanently settled in the region. They intermarried with the Wallche and Yamana populations, and later with arriving Illypnian settlers, forming a minor but incredibly enduring Sanese genetic and cultural strand in Southwest Balisca. This pre-modern episode provides a legendary origin point for the broader Sanese-Baliscan community, which would expand significantly after Balisca's 1808 independence and the subsequent arrival of official contract laborers.

In traditional Okaiken historiography, Takemoto long served as a strict cautionary tale of personal excess and the inevitable ruin that follows rebellion against the rigid Honda order. However, in modern literature, manga, and the theatrical world of Puroresu, he is heavily romanticized as the Drunken Dragon—a charismatic anti-hero who defied the suffocating ''sakoku'' laws through sheer audacity and maritime brilliance. 

In [[Balisca]], Takemoto symbolizes the nation's early multicultural roots and its salad bowl demographic formation. His legacy survives in localized place-name echoes and deep folklore throughout the Tinguiririca region. The historical reality of the Takemoto voyage adds a colorful, eighteenth-century bridge between Okaiken and Balisca, enriching modern diplomatic ties, such as the 2015 Lithium-for-Silicon Pact, with deep historical precedent. It also continues to inspire modern cultural exploration, highlighting isolated geothermal bath traditions and spicy rice dishes that seamlessly blend ancient Sanese and indigenous Baliscan influences.

